WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:10.280
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:13.640
So in this video, we want to
look at experimental studies

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:15.470
of neutrino oscillations.

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:18.710
The first question is, where
do we get the neutrinos?

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:20.180
How do we produce the neutrinos?

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:24.200
The answer is, there's
numerous sources for neutrinos.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.950
You might be lucky and find
them in supernova explosions.

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:30.110
Or if we're really
trying hard, we

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:33.230
can observe them as
relics of the Big Bang.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:36.860
There is a lot of neutrinos
as a relic of the Big

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:39.148
Bang around us.

00:00:39.148 --> 00:00:40.940
Problem is that they
have very low energies

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.400
and are difficult to observe.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:47.930
Easier-- so is the use of
neutrinos in the-- generated

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:50.620
in cosmic ray showers.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.630
There's a lot of neutrinos
coming from the sun.

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:56.990
Beams, beamlines--
accelerators can

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:59.660
be used to smash
particles into a material,

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:04.050
and then in the decay product,
produce also neutrinos.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.519
And also, reactors.

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:09.150
Nuclear reactors can be
used as neutrino sources.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.190
By the way,
neutrinos can also be

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:15.360
used in order to monitor
the nuclear activity

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.470
around the globe.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:18.970
OK.

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:21.360
Studies of neutrino
oscillations.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.180
So we can make this table here
and ask ourselves, what kind

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:26.980
of-- the experimental
parameters are

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:31.120
the length, the energy, and the
sensitivity to a specific mass

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:31.910
range.

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:33.640
So for the solar
neutrinos, you know

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:35.740
the distance between
the Earth and the sun

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:39.400
is pretty much fixed
to first order.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.230
The energy of the
neutrinos coming out

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:42.700
is in the order of 1 meV.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.540
We are going to
look at the table.

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:48.040
And so the mass range you can
probe is 10 to the minus 10

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.060
in delta m squared.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.530
For atmospheric
neutrinos, they're

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:55.050
produced in the
upper atmosphere, 10

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:57.150
to the 4, 10 to 7 meters.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.800
Energies can range--
have a large range,

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:03.540
let's say 10 to the
2 to 10 to the 5 meV.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.020
And then reactors,
typically meV range.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:11.460
It's kind of the nuclear range
for the neutrino energies.

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.530
And the range is
given by how much

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:17.940
space do you have around or
away from a nuclear reactor.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:19.950
Similarly for accelerators.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:23.550
You build an accelerator or
use an existing accelerator,

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.330
and then you build your
detectors, maybe close to it,

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:28.140
and maybe another one far away.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.600
And that's limited by
the size of our planet

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:35.580
or wherever you want to
build your detectors.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.620
Energy ranges there
depends on the energy

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.120
range of the accelerator.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:44.750
And that is in the order of
10 to the 3, 10 to the 4 meV.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:48.662
So you see that it's actually
rather a straightforward study.

00:02:48.662 --> 00:02:50.370
Also, it's interesting
to see-- and we'll

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:53.760
see this next-- what kind
of flavor of neutrinos,

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.830
and whether or not we
can study neutrinos

00:02:55.830 --> 00:03:00.630
or antineutrinos with other
experiment is important.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:02.260
Let's go through this.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:04.950
So it's been a little bit
of a history in how this all

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.070
occurred.

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:09.900
So the first question is, what
happens to the solar neutrinos?

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:13.600
So solar neutrinos
are basically produced

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:15.907
in the core of the sun,
together with light.

00:03:15.907 --> 00:03:17.490
It turns out that
the light of the sun

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.720
takes about 10,000 years
to come out of the sun,

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:24.580
while the neutrinos
come out immediately.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:29.970
So when first experiments tried
to observe solar neutrinos,

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:32.460
they had to theoretically
estimate how many neutrinos

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.800
to expect, and they saw less.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.530
And so one explanation would
have been, or could have been,

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:40.980
or was, maybe something
happened at the core of the sun

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:43.380
and we just haven't seen it
yet, because the light which

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:48.780
come out of the sun has a
delay of up to 10,000 years.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:52.680
That didn't turn
out to be the case.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:55.160
So here is the spectrum
of the neutrino energies

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:59.360
and the specific sources
of neutrinos from the sun.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:02.630
In our nuclear
physics discussion,

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:05.870
we'll get to the point that we
understand how the neutrino--

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:08.840
how the sun produces energy,
and then some of this

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:10.430
becomes more clear.

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:12.545
The story to take
away at this point

00:04:12.545 --> 00:04:13.670
is that there are certain--

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:17.570
there's several processes in
the sun producing neutrinos.

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:20.000
And they all come with
their characteristic energy

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.790
distribution.

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:26.930
But the bottom line is you
find meV scale neutrinos

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:29.170
from the sun.

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:33.040
There's a soup of
electron neutrinos.

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:34.570
They start interacting
with the sun.

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:37.300
And there's a little bit of
a flavor evolution within--

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.400
when they go through
the material of the sun.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.160
But, you know, what
you want to really do

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:47.070
is look for disappearance
in detectors

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:50.310
which are sensitive
to electron neutrinos.

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:53.640
And that has been done in
a number of experiments.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:55.950
Most famous may be the
Davis experiments which

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:57.630
had a big tank of chlorine.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.340
And in the interaction,
you were looking

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:01.875
for finding argon
in your detector,

00:05:01.875 --> 00:05:03.750
and you just every now
and then went in there

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:06.720
and saw how much argon
was actually produced.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.240
And it turned out that those
experiments, all of them,

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:15.450
found a reduced number of
neutrinos, reduced with respect

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:18.130
to the theoretical expectation.

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:21.240
So far so good.

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:22.830
The assumption was that--

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:26.130
or there was no knowledge of
neutrino oscillations or mixing

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:27.540
at this time.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:29.430
So that needed to be explained.

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:31.050
And one way to explain--

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:33.270
it's not just using the
charge interaction, which

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:36.570
allows you to probe the
flavor of the neutrino,

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:37.860
but also lose a neutral--

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:42.990
the neutral
scattering, which then

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:46.140
allows you to measure the
total number of neutrinos.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:48.870
And if you do this-- this was
done by the SNO experiment--

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:51.120
you find that the total
number of neutrinos

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:54.780
is in good agreement with
the theoretical expectation.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:58.970
Hence, those neutrinos
are not really lost,

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:02.570
they're just more from
one flavor into the next.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:09.350
So this was the first
evidence for solar neutrinos

00:06:09.350 --> 00:06:13.850
to be oscillating.

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:17.930
By now there's-- this first
experiment was Homestake.

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:21.080
By now, there is a larger number
of solar neutrino experiments,

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:26.573
and you see the long
time of neutrino studies.

00:06:26.573 --> 00:06:28.740
Different materials are
being used, different energy

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:30.720
thresholds being
tested, different scale

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:32.730
of the experiments, and
experiments become more

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:34.990
sensitive the larger they are.

00:06:34.990 --> 00:06:36.500
And so this you can--

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:39.470
something you can
see from this table.

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:42.220
The next sort of neutrinos
is the ones which

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:44.240
are produced in the atmosphere.

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:47.350
So they are produced in
decays of pions and kaons

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:52.780
and by the cosmic rays
interact with the atmosphere,

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:54.500
or the Earth's atmosphere.

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:55.990
And so you find,
for example, a pi

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:59.350
plus decaying into a
muon and a muon neutrino.

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:01.600
And then the muon
itself can decay

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:04.630
into an electron, an
electron neutrino, and a muon

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:06.070
antineutrino.

00:07:06.070 --> 00:07:10.870
So if you, for example, build
a ratio of muon/antimuon over

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:12.960
electron/anti-electron
neutrinos,

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:16.990
you find it should be around 2.

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:18.910
You have two neutrinos--
muon neutrinos here,

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:21.920
and an electron neutrino.

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:26.520
And also, this wasn't
really observed.

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.530
And you can see
here, as a function

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:31.110
of the column of the
zenith, of looking up

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:33.420
upwards towards the
atmosphere or downwards,

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:38.760
you find that there is an effect
of this kind of oscillation.

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:44.390
So the actual measurement
depends on the energy range.

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:46.740
And you can see that
the muon neutrinos,

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:49.110
the muon-like neutrinos,
they disappear.

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:51.745
You see here in this very
clear plot the prediction

00:07:51.745 --> 00:07:54.120
without oscillation compared
to the experimental results,

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:58.061
so you see the muon
neutrinos actually disappear.

00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:03.320
Moving on, accelerators
can be used.

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:07.820
And the big accelerator on the
Earth at CERN or at Fermilab.

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:14.550
The beamline at Fermilab is
called NuMI, Fermilab National

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:18.210
Accelerator Laboratory, FNAL.

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:21.780
Or CERN, or in Japan.

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:27.740
Those are the big sources of
accelerator-driven neutrinos.

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:34.340
And with those, there's big
detectors, typically a detector

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:38.000
very close to the accelerator
and one further away.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.360
The close one probes
the total flux

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:44.000
of the neutrino at the
experiment, and then the one

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:47.240
which is away in order to probe
the effect of the neutrino

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:51.590
oscillation in order to study
appearance or disappearance.

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:54.110
And again here, you see
this is a long program.

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:58.040
But it basically took off quite
a bit in the 2000s and after.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:00.860
So a lot of neutrino physics
happened in those years.

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:02.540
A lot of information
about the neutrino

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:05.520
was gathered in those years.

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:09.420
And again here-- this is
from the T2K experiment--

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:13.320
you see the comparison between
unoscillated predictions

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:16.890
and oscillated using some
additional constraints

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:20.680
about expectation of the
total flux of the neutrinos,

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:21.930
and that compared to the data.

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:25.320
And you see very
clearly that the--

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:27.125
that the neutrinos
oscillate, that there

00:09:27.125 --> 00:09:29.020
is evidence of oscillation.

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:32.230
All right.

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:35.100
The last source are
reactor neutrinos.

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:38.680
We'll talk about nuclear
physics starting from next week.

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:41.500
Here neutrinos are
produced in nuclear fission

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:46.525
of heavy isotopes, mainly
uranium and polonium.

00:09:46.525 --> 00:09:49.315
The flux can be calculated
in various ways,

00:09:49.315 --> 00:09:52.900
for example by knowing
the nuclear processes

00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:58.010
and the thermal power
produced in the reactor,

00:09:58.010 --> 00:10:00.800
or by just looking at
how much fuel is being--

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:04.910
nuclear fuel is being used
by the reactor itself.

00:10:04.910 --> 00:10:08.510
What's being studied here is
the anti-electron neutrino

00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:09.350
disappearance.

00:10:09.350 --> 00:10:13.370
And what you do here is you use
this inverse beta decay, where

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:16.280
you have a collision
or scattering

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:19.910
of a anti-electron
neutrino with a proton,

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:23.570
creating an electron-- a
positron and a neutron.

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:25.700
And again, there's a
number of experiments.

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:33.560
Basically, whenever you have
a large neutrino experiment,

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:37.850
it can probe surrounding
nuclear reactors.

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:42.350
There's many of them in France
and Japan, also in China.

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:44.510
And they're being used
in those experiments.

00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:48.260
Again, you see that this topic
became really hot in the 2000s.

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:51.650
And again, a lot of--
a lot has been learned.

00:10:51.650 --> 00:10:54.630
So this part here shows you
as a function of the energy--

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:56.270
the length over the energy--

00:10:56.270 --> 00:10:58.400
so kilometer over meV--

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:00.920
the oscillation, the
survival probability,

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:03.800
meaning that you can actually
see directly the oscillation

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:06.090
of the neutrinos.

